## Proof

We compute the degree of the linear map $S: \mathbb{R}^{m+n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m+n}$, $S(x,y) = (y,x)$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}^m$ and $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$.

**Setup.** The degree of a smooth map $f: M \to N$ between connected, oriented, compact $k$-manifolds is defined (at a regular value $q$) by
$$\deg(f) = \sum_{p \in f^{-1}(q)} \operatorname{sgn}\det(df_p),$$
where the sign is $+1$ if $df_p$ sends an oriented basis of $T_pM$ to an oriented basis of $T_qN$, and $-1$ otherwise.

For a linear isomorphism $T: \mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}^k$ (viewed as a map of the oriented manifold $\mathbb{R}^k$ with its standard orientation), every point is a regular value, each fiber is a single point, and the derivative at every point is $T$ itself. Hence
$$\deg(T) = \operatorname{sgn}\det(T).$$

So it suffices to compute $\det S$.

**Computing the determinant via oriented bases.** Equip $\mathbb{R}^{m+n}$ with the standard oriented basis
$$\mathcal{B} = (e_1, \dots, e_m, f_1, \dots, f_n),$$
where $e_i$ spans the $x$-factor $\mathbb{R}^m$ and $f_j$ spans the $y$-factor $\mathbb{R}^n$.

The map $S$ sends
$$e_i \mapsto f_i \text{ (placed in the } x\text{-slot, i.e. the } i\text{-th standard basis vector of the target's first factor)}, \qquad f_j \mapsto e_j \text{ (placed in the } y\text{-slot)}.$$

Concretely, writing coordinates as $(x_1,\dots,x_m,y_1,\dots,y_n)$, the map is
$$(x_1,\dots,x_m,y_1,\dots,y_n) \mapsto (y_1,\dots,y_n,x_1,\dots,x_m).$$

The matrix of $S$ in the standard basis is the permutation matrix of the permutation $\sigma \in S_{m+n}$ that reorders $(1,\dots,m,m+1,\dots,m+n)$ to $(m+1,\dots,m+n,1,\dots,m)$, i.e. $\sigma(i) = i+n$ for $1 \le i \le m$ and $\sigma(m+j) = j$ for $1 \le j \le n$.

**Sign of the permutation.** This permutation moves each of the first $m$ entries past each of the last $n$ entries. Equivalently, it is realized by swapping adjacent elements: each $e_i$ must cross all $n$ entries $f_1,\dots,f_n$. The total number of adjacent transpositions is $mn$, so
$$\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma) = (-1)^{mn}.$$

Since the determinant of a permutation matrix equals the sign of the permutation,
$$\det S = (-1)^{mn}.$$

**Conclusion.** Therefore
$$\deg(S) = \operatorname{sgn}\det(S) = (-1)^{mn}.$$

In particular, $S$ is orientation-preserving when $mn$ is even and orientation-reversing when $mn$ is odd.

